    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, serving stacks combine them: TP within a node (NVLink domain) for dense layers and attention, EP across nodes for MoE layers, and **data parallelism (DP)** replicating the whole TP+EP group to increase throughput. The dominant cost in both TP and EP is **communication**, so the design goal is to overlap or minimize collectives while keeping numerics deterministic enough for validation.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block. For a world size `T` (TP degree):\n\n**Attention block:**\n- Query/Key/Value projections `W_q, W_k, W_v` are **column-parallel**: each rank holds `W_q[:, shard]` etc., producing a partial head group. Heads are partitioned so rank `i` owns heads `[i*H/T, (i+1)*H/T)`.\n- Output projection `W_o` is **row-parallel**: each rank holds `W_o[shard, :]`, consuming its local attention output.\n\n**MLP block:**\n- `W_up` (gate/up projections) are column-parallel: each rank computes `Y_i = X \u00b7 W_up[:, shard_i]` and the local activation `\u03c3(...) \u2299 ...` element",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
